  <div class="document">
    <div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Even Hu'ezer</div>
    <div class="section-title">Kesuvos [Laws of the Marriage Contract] — §2</div>

    <div class="halachic-ruling">
      The matter of the <em>kesuvah</em> — based on what Rabbeinu, of blessed memory, wrote in the teaching <b><em>"Kartalisah"</em></b> in <em>Likutay Moharan</em> Part One <span class="xref">(§18)</span>. See the entire teaching there.
    </div>

    <div class="english-block">

      <p>For everything has a <em>tachlis</em> (purpose, ultimate completion), and the <em>tachlis</em> is bound to the <em>machshavah</em> (the original thought) and is closer to the <em>machshavah</em> than the intermediate action from which the <em>tachlis</em> comes — in the dimension of <b><em>sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah</em></b> (the end of the deed was in the beginning of the thought) <span class="xref">(from the <em>Lecha Dodi</em> liturgy)</span>. And the principal <em>tachlis</em> of all Creation is the delight of the World to Come. This <em>tachlis</em> can only be attained — <em>tzaddikim</em> alone attain this <em>tachlis</em> — each one according to his root within the soul of the <em>tzaddik</em>, from which he receives this <em>tachlis</em> in proportion to his ability to transform anger into compassion, and so forth.</p>

      <p>And the general principle is: through the <em>tzaddikim</em> receiving the dimension of <em>Malchus</em> (Kingship) and leadership, the <em>tzaddikim</em> thereby know the <em>tachlis</em>. For through their grasp of the dimension of <em>Malchus</em>, they thereby hold fast to the wholeness of the letters within <em>Malchus</em>, and through holding fast to the complete letters, they thereby hold fast to the <em>tachlis</em> — which is the completeness of the letters, which is the dimension of <em>Yud</em>, the dimension of the World to Come, and so forth. See there carefully.</p>

      <p>And this is the dimension of <span class="trans"><em>"B'chol derachecha da'eihu"</em></span> — in all your ways, know Him <span class="xref">(Mishlei 3:6)</span> — through which a person can attain the <em>tachlis</em> in everything. For every thing was created through letters, and so forth. And the <em>tachlis</em> is called <em>tekheles</em> (the blue-violet color of the <em>tzitzis</em> thread), for <em>tekheles</em> is a blend of black and white — like writing, which is black upon white; certainly the underside of the letter, and so forth — for so it is that the <em>tachlis</em>, which is the end of the deed (<em>sof ha'ma'aseh</em>), is in the beginning of the thought (<em>machshavah techilah</em>) and is close to the thought, as above. See all of this there very carefully, for these are exceedingly deep matters.</p>

      <hr class="divider">

      <p>And this is the dimension of the <em>kesuvah</em> — that one must write a <em>kesuvah</em> for one's wife, and without a <em>kesuvah</em> it is forbidden to dwell with her. For in all things that a person does, one must keep an eye on the <em>tachlis</em>. And this is the above-mentioned dimension of <span class="trans"><em>"b'chol derachecha da'eihu"</em></span> — for the <em>tachlis</em> is the essential thing, and the essence of the <em>tachlis</em> is in the dimension of <em>sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah</em> — namely, that the end of the deed should be bound and included within the beginning of the thought. This is the core intention of Creation, and this is the essential <em>tachlis</em> of all of Creation in its entirety.</p>

      <p>And it is known that the essential attainment of the <em>tachlis</em> depends specifically upon <b><em>shmiyas habris</em></b> (guarding the holy covenant), which is in the dimension of <em>Malchus</em>. For through this, the <em>tzaddikim</em> who guard the covenant merit the dimension of <em>Malchus</em> and thereby attain the <em>tachlis</em> through the dimension of <em>Malchus</em>, as above. For the essential <em>tachlis</em> — which is the dimension of <em>sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah</em> — comes specifically through the dimension of <em>Malchus</em>, since <em>Malchus</em> is in the dimension of <em>sof ha'ma'aseh</em> as is known, and it is the dimension of <em>sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah</em> — in the dimension of <b><em>Keser Malchus</em></b>: <em>Malchus</em> at the beginning and <em>Malchus</em> at the end, in the dimension of <span class="trans"><em>"Ani rishon va'ani acharon"</em></span> — I am the first and I am the last <span class="xref">(Yeshayahu 44:6)</span> — as brought in the sacred books. And as Eliyahu said: <em>Keser Malchus</em> — of it is stated <span class="trans"><em>"Magid mei'reishis acharis"</em></span> — He declares the end from the beginning <span class="xref">(Yeshayahu 46:10)</span>.</p>

      <p>For the primary purpose of Creation was to reveal His Kingship (<em>Malchus</em>). It emerges, then, that <em>Malchus</em> is the dimension of <em>sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah</em>: the revelation of <em>Malchus</em> can only occur after Creation, since <span class="trans">there is no King without a people</span> — it is therefore in the dimension of <em>sof ha'ma'aseh</em>, for <em>Malchus</em> can only be revealed at the end of the deed, that is, when all of Creation is complete. Yet it is in the <em>machshavah techilah</em>, for the beginning of the thought behind all of Creation was for no other purpose than this — to reveal His Kingship. And this is the essential <em>tachlis</em>: when one merits that the end of the deed returns and is included within the beginning of the thought — namely, that the dimension of <em>Malchus</em>, which is the dimension of the totality of Creation, the dimension of <em>sof ha'ma'aseh</em>, returns and is included in its root in the beginning of the thought. This is the essential <em>tachlis</em>, as above — understand this well.</p>

      <p>And all of this depends specifically upon <em>shmiyas habris</em> (guarding the covenant), which is the foundation of the entire Torah. And this is the essential dimension of the holy <em>zivoug</em> (conjugal union) — to bind and connect the end of the deed with the beginning of the thought. For <span class="trans"><em>ishah yir'as Hashem</em></span> — a woman who fears Hashem <span class="xref">(Mishlei 31:30)</span> — this is in the dimension of <em>Malchus</em>, the dimension of <em>sof ha'ma'aseh</em>. And through the holy <em>zivoug</em> performed as it should be, the end of the deed is joined and included in the beginning of the thought. Through this one is called by the name <em>tzaddik</em> through <em>shmiyas habris</em>, and one merits attaining the <em>tachlis</em>, as above. And the reverse — God forbid — one whose union is not in holiness, God forbid, and who blemishes the covenant, God forbid, is in the dimension of <span class="trans"><em>"v'nirgan mafried aluf"</em></span> — a slanderer separates an intimate friend <span class="xref">(Mishlei 16:28)</span> — for he causes separation, God forbid, between the end of the deed and the beginning of the thought.</p>

      <p>And therefore the blemish of the covenant is called <em>zenus</em> (licentiousness), in the language of <em>hefsek</em> (break, interruption) and <em>yotzei lachutz</em> (going outside) — as Rashi explained on the verse <span class="trans"><em>"atah yiznu saz'nusehah"</em></span> — "now her licentiousness has gone out from her" <span class="xref">(Yechezkel 23)</span> — for <em>zenus</em>, the blemish of the covenant, God forbid, is the dimension of separation (<em>perud</em>), break (<em>hefsek</em>), and going outside (<em>yotzei lachutz</em>): it does not bind and include the end of the deed within the beginning of the thought — on the contrary, it separates, God forbid, between the end of the deed and the beginning of the thought.</p>

      <hr class="divider">

      <p>And this is what our Sages of blessed memory enacted: to give a <em>kesuvah</em> to one's wife so that there should be a complete bond between them — as our Sages of blessed memory said, so that she should not be light in his eyes to divorce her. For through the <em>kesuvah</em>, a bond is created between husband and wife — who are in the dimension of <em>sof ma'aseh</em> and <em>machshavah techilah</em> as is known — that is, they are bound and included together in the dimension of <em>sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah</em>. This is the dimension of the <em>tachlis</em> upon which the essential holiness of the union depends, as above.</p>

      <p>For <em>kesav</em> (writing) is in the dimension of the <em>tachlis</em> — in the dimension of the union of black and white, the dimension of <em>tekheles</em> — as is explained there in the above-mentioned teaching. See there. Therefore specifically through <em>kesav</em> — that is, the <em>kesuvah</em> — through this specifically they are joined in the dimension of holy <em>zivoug</em>, which is in the dimension of the <em>tachlis</em>, in the dimension of <em>sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah</em>, which is the dimension of <em>kesav</em>, as above.</p>

      <p>And therefore he gives her the <em>kesuvah</em> in the form of a <b>monetary obligation</b> he undertakes toward her — for wealth and money are in the dimension of <em>sof ha'ma'aseh</em>, since money is essentially in the dimension of <em>asiyah</em> (action, doing), as is understood from Rabbeinu's words elsewhere <span class="xref">(LM §17)</span>, that money is in the dimension of <em>asiyah</em>. And the root of money is in the dimension of the <em>machshavah techilah</em> — in the dimension of <span class="trans"><em>mistitra diyemina mocha chivra k'kaspa</em></span> — from the right side, the white brain as silver <span class="xref">(Zohar)</span>. It emerges, then, that money is in the dimension of <em>sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah</em>. Therefore, through the money he obligates himself toward her in the <em>kesuvah</em>, they are thereby bound in the dimension of <em>sof ma'aseh b'machshavah techilah</em>. This is the dimension of the <em>tachlis</em> upon which the essential holiness of the union depends, as above.</p>

      <p>For the essential <em>tachlis</em> is attained by the <em>tzaddikim</em> through the dimension of <span class="trans"><em>kartalisah d'ittesa d'Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa da'asidah l'mishdi beih tekhelesah</em></span> — the basket of Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa's wife, which in the future is destined to cast its <em>tekheles</em> within it <span class="xref">(LM §18)</span>. See there carefully in the above-mentioned teaching — that is, <span class="trans"><em>ishah yir'as Hashem</em></span>, the God-fearing woman, as above.</p>

      <p>And therefore one is obligated to give her money and wealth in her <em>kesuvah</em>. For when the end of the deed is bound and included within the beginning of the thought — which is the dimension of the holy <em>zivoug</em>, as above — then life and an abundance of <em>shefa</em> (divine flow) is drawn from the beginning of the thought to the end of the deed, as is known. And this is the dimension of the wealth he is obligated toward her in the <em>kesuvah</em> — the dimension of the <em>shefa</em> of wealth that flows to the end of the deed through its being included in the beginning of the thought.</p>

      <p>And therefore it is forbidden to dwell with one's wife without a <em>kesuvah</em> — so that the union should not be, God forbid, in the dimension of separation (<em>perud</em>), which is the dimension of <em>zenus</em> — that is, so that the end of the deed should not be unincluded, God forbid, in the beginning of the thought (which is the dimension of <em>kesav</em>), for then no wealth flows to the end of the deed, God forbid, as above. Therefore one is obligated to give her a <em>kesuvah</em> so that the bond should be in the dimension of <em>kesav</em>, in the dimension of the <em>tachlis</em>, in the dimension of the comprehensive binding of the end of the deed within the beginning of the thought — through which <em>shefa</em> and wealth flow to the end of the deed, as above. And this is the dimension of the two hundred <em>zuz</em> he is obligated to give her in her <em>kesuvah</em>.</p>

      <p>For two hundred <em>zuz</em> is the dimension of wealth — as our Sages of blessed memory said <span class="xref">(Pe'ah, Chapter 8, Mishnah 8)</span>: one who possesses two hundred <em>zuz</em> may not take from charity. Thus two hundred <em>zuz</em> is the dimension of wealth — for wealth flows to the end of the deed through its being included in the beginning of the thought, which is the dimension of <em>kesav</em>, the dimension of <em>kesuvah</em>, as above.</p>

      <p class="source-attr">Blessed is Hashem forever, Amen and Amen.</p>

